WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.770
BOB ANDERSON: Thank you
all for coming tonight.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.960
I'm Bob Anderson.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.480
And on behalf of the MIT
Enterprise Forum of Cambridge,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.680
I want to welcome you all to
this evening's presentation

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.840
of the 50K Competition
final event.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.820
The MIT Enterprise
Forum of Cambridge

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.450
has been hosting the 50K final
event since it started in 1990.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.710
We are thrilled to highlight
the extraordinary talent, ideas,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.920
and enthusiasm for
entrepreneurship that exist

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.410
within the MIT student body.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.470
The Forum's mission-- to help
entrepreneurs build successful

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:48.700
companies--

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.040
fits well with the
student run activity.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.320
And the forum will be a
resource for these teams

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.260
once they've graduated and
begun the serious business

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.740
of building a company.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.860
Our jumpstart clinics,
startup clinics,

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.790
[? 10250s, ?] spring
and fall workshops,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:07.680
and technology briefing
series all help entrepreneurs.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.400
The MIT Enterprise
Forum of Cambridge

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.580
is celebrating its 20th
anniversary this year.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.850
Begun as an educational
service to MIT alumni,

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.380
the forum has
broadened its reach

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.390
to include not only
the MIT community,

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.170
but the entire entrepreneurial
community of New England.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.740
There are also 18 enterprise
forum chapters worldwide,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.100
all dedicated to promoting the
growth of technology oriented

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.710
companies.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.330
And in fact, they're
often linked.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.940
On May 21, Professor
Bob Langer will

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.590
be giving a satellite
broadcast out

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.600
of [? Crusky. ?] More
information on that

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.690
is in the brochures
at the entrance.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.620
Here in the Boston
area, these teams

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.250
also find a great
infrastructure of resources

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.050
to help them build
their companies.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.020
One example are the
volunteers and the sponsors

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.760
of the MIT Enterprise Forum.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.830
I want to thank
tonight's sponsor,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.740
Goodwin, Procter, and Hoar,
for their long-term support,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.837
both of the forum and
entrepreneurs in general.

00:02:01.837 --> 00:02:03.420
You should all have
a fun time tonight

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.510
I extend my congratulations
to all the 50K entrepreneurs,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:08.970
especially tonight's finalists.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:09.470
Thank you.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.264
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:54.750
Welcome everybody to the ninth
annual MIT 50K Entrepreneurship

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:55.620
Competition.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.550
So what's on tap for tonight?

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.140
[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.410
PRESENTER: What is
on tap for tonight?

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.330
First we want to talk
for a moment about what

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.660
is the MIT 50K for some
of you who haven't run

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.610
into the competition before.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.950
Then we're really excited
to have Bill Porter,

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.980
founder and chairman of E*Trade
[? Group ?] [? Inc. ?] coming

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.260
to share some words of advice
with these teams who are

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.510
launching their ventures.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.790
Next we'd like to
introduce the organizing

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.680
team that will be running
the competition next year.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.520
And finally, the part that
we're all here for tonight,

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.160
each of the finalists teams will
be presenting their business

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.130
ideas for you, and
you can be the judge.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:45.630
And at the end of
the evening, we'll

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.960
find out who are judging
panel chose this year's 50K

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.070
grand prize and
runner up winners.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.220
First, before we continue, we'd
like to thank our sponsors.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.020
Our sponsors give
us the money that it

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.150
takes to run the
competition every year

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.580
in addition to the
$50,000 worth of money

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.040
we give out as prizes each year.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.860
We also take about 50 to $60,000
to put on each of the programs.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.630
We have several events sponsors.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.300
Motorola New Ventures sponsors
our team building dinners

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.260
each month.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.570
Testa, Hurwitz, and Thibeault
sponsors the Intellectual

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:20.100
Property Seminar.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:23.280
Polaroid sponsors our Ideas
and Innovation Seminar.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.470
And McKinsey and Company
sponsored our first

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.840
ever Global Startup
Workshop for organizers

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.720
of business plan competitions.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.610
We also have many
other sponsors who

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.740
provide the services,
the money that it

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.380
takes to keep our lights
running and our office going

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.380
and our faxes going
out every year.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.217
And we have
sponsorship from alumni

00:06:42.217 --> 00:06:43.800
who have given back
to the competition

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.400
Silicon Space, our inclined
sponsors who provide services

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.920
to the teams coming
out of the competition,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.100
and very importantly,
our sponsors within MIT,

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:58.370
including all the groups here
and MIT Resource Development.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.050
So what are the steps of the
50K we start in the fall.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.850
We had 1K warm-up round.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.040
We had 120 entries in that.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:09.520
The judges selected 10
teams to identify and honor

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.530
as teams showing great promise.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.350
We came back in the
spring with our kickoff,

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.530
the executive summaries
and semifinalist plans due.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:20.575
And tonight, we're here
at the 50K final awards.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.160
So we've seen the process.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.850
But what are the results
of this network, process,

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.680
and resources?

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.740
In the past nine years,
over 30 companies

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.600
have come out of
the competition.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.170
They've created over 500 jobs
and have an aggregate market

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:42.200
value of over $180 million.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.040
And it's not just these
large aggregate numbers.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.510
It's stories like Virtual Ink,
a 1997 winner of the competition

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:53.950
just received $1 million
worth of seed investment.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.560
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:58.600
They've recently moved
into office space

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.030
with one of our
sponsors, Polaroid,

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.840
and will be launching
their product in Q4 in '98.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:05.830
Right?

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.200
Stylus Innovations
sold their company

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:14.080
that won the competition in
1991 for $12.8 million in 1995.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.260
Web Line, a 1996 winner,
received $7 million

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.830
worth of venture
capital last year.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.540
And most interestingly,
two of our alumni, Flash

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:27.876
Communications and Firefly, were
both purchased by Microsoft.

00:08:27.876 --> 00:08:29.360
[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:30.070
You be the judge.

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:35.586
The finalists that you see
here tonight are tremendous.

00:08:35.586 --> 00:08:37.419
But they are not the
only ones going forward

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:38.530
to launch their ventures.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.966
And tonight's is a
celebration, a commencement

00:08:40.966 --> 00:08:42.549
of the ventures for
all the teams that

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:44.350
have participated in the 50K.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.600
I'd like to take a moment
and have each of these teams

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.520
stand up so that you
can identify them,

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.120
seek them out afterwards,
and say hello.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.755
Beacon Software.

00:08:53.755 --> 00:08:54.880
Can you turn on the lights?

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:05.260
Beacon Software, Exotech,
Biomedical Modeling,

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:09.420
Frictionless Commerce,
Catalyst Software,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.250
[? Net ?] [? Pecker, ?]
Connected Care,

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.480
[? Netican, ?]
Cortical Technologies,

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.730
Past Technologies,
Ethos Technologies,

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.532
and [? Vert ?] [? Med. ?]

00:09:21.532 --> 00:09:24.448
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:36.548 --> 00:09:38.340
Just have to get my
notes so I don't forget

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.632
anything really important here.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:46.140
Now I'm very pleased to
introduce to you Bill Porter.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.750
He's the founder and chairman
of E*Trade Group as we mentioned

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:52.315
earlier and near and dear to the
heart of MIT since he's class

00:09:52.315 --> 00:09:52.815
of '67.

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:59.040
He founded the E*Trade company,
formerly known as E*Trade Plus

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.140
in 1982.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.320
Prior to founding E*Trade, Mr.
Porter held research management

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.040
positions at General
Electric and Textron.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.060
He received a BA in mathematics
from Adams State College,

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.180
an MA in physics from
Kansas State College,

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:19.320
and an MBA in management
from the MIT Sloan School.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.960
Mr. Porter retired as
chief executive officer

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:24.270
in April 1996.

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:28.440
In May 1996, Mr. Porter was
named Silicon Valley's emerging

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:32.360
company entrepreneur of the year
by San Jose Business Journal.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:35.220
And most excitingly,
Mr. Porter has just

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:36.960
founded a company,
which is operating

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.020
in stealth mode, that promises
to be very, very exciting.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:41.520
Bill Porter.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:59.440
BILL PORTER: Thank you.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.760
Well, I feel pretty
humbled to be here at MIT.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.870
You know, the intellectual
stimulation at MIT

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.840
is not surpassed any
place that I know.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:14.890
There are so many bright people
on the staff or on the faculty.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:19.390
And the students here are
just absolutely outstanding.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:23.290
And so all I can do is
tell it like I see it,

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:24.560
and I will do just that.

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:30.510
What I'm going to do is
talk just a little bit about

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:31.010
E*Trade.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:32.150
And then I have
some other things

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.130
that I would just like
to pass on as ideas

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.850
about entrepreneurship.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:45.560
We originated online
brokerage in 1982.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:48.080
We were the very
first ones to do that.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.790
And I started with
another fellow.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.350
And we spent 10 years
getting it going.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.350
And so it takes a
lot of perseverance

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.800
to make things happen.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.360
And finally, we founded E*Trade
the beginning of this decade.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:04.400
And during the '80s, we actually
serviced Schwab, Fidelity,

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:08.090
Quick &amp; Reilly, and a number
of other broker dealers

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:11.840
where we were basically
a service bureau.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:17.990
And we formed E*Trade as such
in 1992, which was only-- what--

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:22.250
five years ago or six
years ago, I guess it was.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:24.350
And we basically
have led the market

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.570
in terms of price decrease.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:28.490
I established the
strategy early on

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:31.070
to decrease the price as
a function of our volumes

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:34.580
to maintain profitability
and with a total objective

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:39.930
of obtaining essentially
market recognition

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:43.970
and be the brand leader,
which we have achieved.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.090
And there's our
six years growth.

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:48.590
The overall growth-- I forget
in the first couple of months,

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:52.220
which was infinite, of course--

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:55.310
the overall growth
month to month averages

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:59.630
today for the whole
six years 8% a month.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:01.040
And you'll see a
little dip there

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.700
at the tail end of last year
and the beginning of this year.

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:06.778
As you may remember toward
the end of last year,

00:13:06.778 --> 00:13:08.070
there was a dump in the market.

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:11.460
And sure enough, our market
decreased accordingly.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.370
But people come
right back after--

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:18.020
they forget those things
very quickly, thank goodness.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:22.010
And of course, we're in a
very hot market as well.

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:25.880
Well, we have arrived
at a point where we

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.350
have very competitive prices.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:30.050
There are some people that
are lower than we are.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:33.800
But we're satisfied with
where we're at for right now.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.140
And it's a very
competitive situation.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:37.670
And people do a
lot of trading when

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.820
it's this inexpensive to trade.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:44.760
Now, as an aside, just one of
those little gems of knowledge

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.997
if you ever want to pass
it on, one of the things,

00:13:47.997 --> 00:13:49.580
you'll notice that
the prices that you

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:54.920
see for online brokerage is all
like, you know, 14.95 or 19.95,

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.430
or 7.95, et cetera, et cetera.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:13:58.910
Where the $0.05 come from?

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:00.875
Well, as I mentioned,
you know, I

00:14:00.875 --> 00:14:02.280
price decreased over the years.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.920
In fact, I cut
prices seven times

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.560
since, I think it
was about four years.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:12.650
And in doing that, we
were let's say $30 or $35.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:16.370
And so then I'd bounce
it down to 30 from 35.

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:20.240
And so at one
transition of that type,

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.080
one of my stockholders
of the company

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:26.173
said let's have
our annual meeting.

00:14:26.173 --> 00:14:27.590
Let's just get
together for lunch.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:31.012
So OK fine, so Steve came
over, and we went out to lunch.

00:14:31.012 --> 00:14:32.720
And I said, Steve,
guess what, on Monday,

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.137
I'm announcing the
fact that we're

00:14:34.137 --> 00:14:36.530
going to go from $35 to $30.

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:37.880
He said, no, no Bill.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:40.760
He says go to 29.95.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.040
So I said, OK,
that's a good idea.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:44.780
Mind you this guy
was from car parts

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:48.100
business, very successful.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:50.610
He knew how to do
the merchandising.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:52.650
So I adopted the 29.95.

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.730
And the whole
industry has followed.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:59.160
Now, you might be interested
to know that I have calculated

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:03.360
that that $0.05 per trade
amounts to about $400,000

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.340
a year to the industry.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:09.020
That's a lot of nickels.

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:13.050
Anyway, the customer
acceptance, we

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:16.620
have about 450,000 accounts,
$10 billion in customers.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:20.280
But do we do about 45,000
trades a day currently.

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:23.090
We have cracked
the $50,000 mark.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:28.290
I'm sorry, 50,000 trademark,
which is a lot of trading.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:30.570
And we've done over
10 million trades

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:35.790
to date without any
real serious problems.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:42.810
We have 9% to 10% or pardon
me 8% to 9% per month growth.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:45.750
And we were ranked as the
fastest growing company

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.670
in Silicon Valley.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:51.325
And on top of that,
we have about 200--

00:15:51.325 --> 00:15:52.950
this is from the last
quarter, so we're

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.200
a little higher than this now.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.850
Shouldn't say that, but I will.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:16:02.790
204 million annualized revenues
and 20% pre-tax 200 million

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:06.700
in cash.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.730
And we're saving that
cash, because there's

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:10.530
a lot of new entrants.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:12.210
They're not all
going to make it.

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:15.390
And come a drop in the
market, they'll be hurting.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:17.790
And our plan is acquisition.

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:21.510
And that's the reason we
built this work chest of cash.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.180
And we have over
a billion dollars

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:29.670
in assets, no debt whatsoever,
about 750 employees.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.350
But again, we're
growing at 8% a month.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.900
So that number is very dynamic.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:38.120
And we had an IPO a year
and a half ago at about $10,

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:40.470
and our stock price
today is about $25

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.200
with a P/E of about 60.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.920
Now I want to turn to a totally
different story, because I

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:52.500
want to make a point with it for
the entrepreneurs in the room.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:54.360
I had an idea.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:56.820
And while I was in college,
I didn't have any money,

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:00.230
so I had to work on
the railroad at night

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:03.900
on the D&amp;RG out in Colorado.

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:07.930
And I noticed that the
roundhouse workers, the fellows

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.810
that worked in the roundhouse,
they understood steam engines,

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.990
but they didn't know what a
diesel electric locomotive was.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:18.210
And on top of that, they
were very highly unionized.

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:21.912
They had an attitude of,
well, very highly unionized.

00:17:21.912 --> 00:17:22.829
I'll leave it at that.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:26.040
OK.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:30.510
Then in about 1965 when I
was working as an R&amp;D manager

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:34.140
at Textron, I found
that as a company,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.290
we were producing automotive--

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:39.030
or pardon me, checkout
equipment for aircraft.

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:41.820
And it struck me, why
not see if maybe there's

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:45.780
some application here on
diesel electric locomotives?

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:49.800
And so I took that idea.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:54.260
And I took it to the
Southern Pacific Railroad.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:57.900
And there was a fellow there
who was actually the finance VP.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:06.010
And he and I got together at MIT
and proceeded with the concept.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:08.010
And so we started a
development program

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:10.770
to produce some automatic
checkout equipment

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:13.140
for diesel electric locomotives.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:15.060
And GM did not like the idea.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:19.000
You can't touch my diesel
engine, et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:22.830
So we had a few battles
to fight, which we won.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:25.300
And the program was
highly successful.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:28.560
And the Southern Pacific's
data at that time

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:32.250
showed that about 20% of
their locomotives, including

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:35.220
locomotives on the
road were not working.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:38.970
In other words, they were
not pulling as they should.

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:44.780
And they estimated,
also, that the Textron--

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:46.950
that's where I was
working at the time--

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:51.128
automatic checkout system
would cut the losses in half.

00:18:51.128 --> 00:18:53.170
And of course, we were
looking for shorts, opens,

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:53.850
and so forth.

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.100
We put a harness
into the locomotive,

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:58.770
the electric locker,
and had a plug-in piece

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:02.230
of equipment sitting beside
the track and so forth.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:05.100
And so we sold the system.

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:08.110
I sold the system to a number
of different railroads,

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.680
about a half a
dozen major roads.

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:14.040
And at the time, there
were 33,000 locomotives

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.200
in operation in the US.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.798
And therefore, I really feel
that I was instrumental.

00:19:18.798 --> 00:19:21.090
And somebody else would have
done it if I hadn't, but I

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:22.170
did it.

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:25.630
And I put 3,000
locomotives on the road.

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:31.050
And that's a-- you know,
I'm kind of proud of this.

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:36.540
And really, I was making,
I think, about $25,000

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.520
a year at that
point in time, which

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:44.050
was more money than is today,
it seems like, but not a lot.

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:47.610
And really, why did I
tell you this story?

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.240
Well, really for two reasons.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.950
And I'm really directing
this to the folks that

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:57.222
are entrepreneurially inclined
and leaving here and so forth.

00:19:57.222 --> 00:19:58.680
And you know, it's
the achievement.

00:19:58.680 --> 00:19:59.847
To me, it's the achievement.

00:19:59.847 --> 00:20:03.880
It's the accomplishment of
doing it that really counts.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:04.380
And--

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:11.295 --> 00:20:12.420
I mean, don't get me wrong.

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:15.940
The money is nice, but it
isn't where it's at, you know.

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:18.120
Don't pay attention how
much money you're making.

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:18.990
Get the job done.

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:20.850
That's what really counts.

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:24.030
And the other thing
is that there's

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:28.590
all kinds of opportunities
for entrepreneurially

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.750
inclined individuals,
who can just

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:34.770
look at things in everyday
life and see things

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:37.780
to be done and go do them.

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:41.490
So there's just no
shortage of things

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:47.050
to be done in our society
for the benefit of society.

00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:49.980
Well, speaking of
opportunities just a little

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:56.280
bit, as I see it in the
coming century and borrowing

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:00.720
on the idea of plate tectonics
and so forth, sources

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:02.250
of energy.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.490
I mean, you know, we're going
to run out of coal and oil

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:07.000
and so forth.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.420
And what are we going
to do to replace it?

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:12.970
And that is a real problem
I see coming in the future.

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:16.772
I remember when I first
left, got my masters

00:21:16.772 --> 00:21:19.230
and went to work for the Bureau
of Standards in Washington.

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.530
I met a fellow who was working
in the Interior Department,

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:25.920
and he said, we're going
to be out of oil in 1975,

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:28.043
I think it's what he said.

00:21:28.043 --> 00:21:29.460
And I don't know
what the estimate

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:33.720
is today is probably the
year 2030, and by 2030 maybe.

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:35.370
But someday, we'll run out.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:38.152
And it's really
important to pursue that.

00:21:38.152 --> 00:21:40.110
And unfortunately, I
don't think our government

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:41.710
does enough in that dimension.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:44.940
But I think there's some
real opportunities there.

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:47.940
And worldwide commerce,
there's no question about it.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:51.490
We are expanding E*Trade, for
example, on a worldwide basis.

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:53.100
And there's all kinds
of opportunities

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:56.310
all over the world, particularly
in the underdeveloped countries

00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:59.160
to help them kick start.

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.980
And you don't necessarily
make money there.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.690
But I think there's
a lot to be said

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:11.460
for the achievement of making
mankind's position better.

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:13.620
Environmental protection,
no question about it.

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:16.560
I think this past century
has really been one of wars.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.380
And I don't think we're going to
have any more significant wars.

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.670
I hope to heck and I'm
sure everybody else does,

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.040
that that is true.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:25.110
But the coming
century has just got

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:27.960
to bear down on the whole
subject of the environment,

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:29.920
no question about it.

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:31.530
I was in Manila recently.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:35.430
And it's just almost
deplorable the situation there

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:37.470
from an environmental
point of view.

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:40.180
And something has to be done.

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:41.740
A lot of things have to be done.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:44.940
And there's opportunity there.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:47.190
The information
age, I hardly have

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:49.650
to say anything about that.

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:53.730
What I see the other day that
the amount of content online

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:56.820
is doubling every 100 days.

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:59.280
And of course, that
makes it doubly difficult

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:02.220
for some of the internet starts
that you hear about and so

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:03.220
forth.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:05.820
But the sorting of that and
the use of that and so forth

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.340
provides all kinds
of opportunities.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:10.680
And actually, you've
heard of disintermediation

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:12.120
and all of the
things that brings

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:13.950
about in terms of opportunity.

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:17.580
But also there's a whole
subject of partnership

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:20.430
and joint venturing between
various organizations

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:21.540
on the internet.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.810
So it just creates all
kinds of opportunity.

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:29.850
But you know, if I could ask the
good Lord to give me 100 years

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:33.090
to watch one science evolve,
it would be this one, the life

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:36.900
science, engineering,
because I really

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:40.350
think that what will happen
in terms of our ability

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:43.530
to change life as
we know it today,

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:47.610
both people and their plants
and foods and everything else,

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:51.270
is just absolutely going to
explode over the lives of you

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:53.880
young folks that are
graduating today.

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:56.400
And I just think
it's a terrific area

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:00.600
to pursue, particularly when you
couple it with the information

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:01.103
age.

00:24:01.103 --> 00:24:03.270
I really think it's going
to change the whole fabric

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:03.895
of our society.

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:08.550
And so just the
combination of those two,

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:13.560
I think is one of the most
exciting areas to pursue.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:16.120
Well, just as one
of those areas,

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:19.210
and I'll just flip through
this quickly, is excess trash.

00:24:19.210 --> 00:24:21.880
You know, my wife and I, our
kids are all grown and gone.

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.400
We fill one to two
trash cans a week.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:26.710
It's absolutely ridiculous.

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:28.590
And most of it comes
from packaging.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.150
And there's all
kinds of things that

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:33.690
could be done in the packaging
domain that are not being done,

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.230
I believe, and still
maintain good marketability

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.150
of the product.

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:42.360
And catalogs, we
probably get two

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:45.000
or three catalogs every day.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.320
It's absolutely ridiculous.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:48.210
And what can be done?

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:50.700
There's opportunities
in recycling,

00:24:50.700 --> 00:24:54.050
produce electricity or
fertilizer with them,

00:24:54.050 --> 00:24:57.600
combine with the internet to
reduce the number of pages

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:02.550
or whatever, thinner
paper, paper substitute,

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:03.870
pass new laws.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:06.490
There's opportunities actually
in Congress and so forth

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:09.420
to do things of an
entrepreneurial nature that

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:12.170
need to be done.

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:14.320
There really just are a
multitude of opportunities.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:18.190
I just illustrate this
with just one dimension.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:20.720
Well, the essential
characteristics,

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:25.610
as I see it of an entrepreneur,
are the key ingredients.

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:29.200
I don't know what you
want to call it exactly is

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:32.070
first of all basic integrity.

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:34.960
And there are so
many people who seem

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:37.660
to lose that in the
business environment

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:38.980
once they get into it.

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:41.290
And they kind of
manipulate things

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:42.800
a little bit and so forth.

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:45.260
And I just don't think
that's the way to believe.

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:47.260
You know, in the long
haul, the only person

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:49.507
you have to report
to is yourself.

00:25:49.507 --> 00:25:52.090
And when you do that, you want
to be able to stand up and say,

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:54.880
I did it right.

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:58.110
And too many people just
go litigious and crazy.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:01.740
And just don't ever do that.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:05.520
That would be rule
number one in my book.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:06.970
And look for the opportunities.

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:08.590
You know, they're
all over the place

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:11.440
of things to change and
new markets, new products,

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:12.640
and services.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.300
And they're very
good for society.

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:20.260
And you know, most of them are
pretty obvious things to do.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:21.430
And do your homework.

00:26:21.430 --> 00:26:24.880
When you have an idea,
really dig into it,

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:27.700
see what the competition
is, what the market is,

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:30.250
what your product is, what it's
going to cost, et cetera, et

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:31.083
cetera.

00:26:31.083 --> 00:26:32.500
You really got to
do your homework

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:35.020
to be absolutely convinced
you're right, even though you

00:26:35.020 --> 00:26:37.810
may be partially wrong.

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:41.260
And when you are certain, there
is no question, go for it.

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:42.970
Don't stand back
and say, well, geez,

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:44.860
you know, somebody
could do to thus and so.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:46.060
Go do it.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:47.410
This is America.

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:50.122
It's OK to go belly
up and start again.

00:26:50.122 --> 00:26:52.330
You know, you can't do that
every place in the world.

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:53.170
But here, you can.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:58.123
And all it really
costs you is maybe

00:26:58.123 --> 00:27:00.040
you have to go out and
get a job for someplace

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.720
else for a little while.

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:04.490
And it's OK to do that.

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:06.130
And stick to your
guns, because there's

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:08.390
going to be many people
that tell you, you're crazy,

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:10.210
you can't do this, et cetera.

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:12.460
If you've done your homework
and are convinced of it,

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.107
just stick to it.

00:27:14.107 --> 00:27:16.690
However, you got to be ready to
make changes when you find out

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:18.522
that, hey, it wasn't
really quite that way,

00:27:18.522 --> 00:27:20.230
and I have to go a
little bit to the left

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:23.060
to get around this corner.

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.240
So you do have to make changes.

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:29.290
But basically, you gotta
plow ahead and stick to it .

00:27:29.290 --> 00:27:32.380
And I don't know if they ever
teach you this in your finance

00:27:32.380 --> 00:27:34.230
class, but cash is king.

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:36.168
You've got to have the
money to get there.

00:27:36.168 --> 00:27:37.210
You've got to protect it.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:38.680
You've got to take
good care of it.

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:40.780
Fancy offices don't
mean an awful lot.

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:45.100
What counts is being able to
meet the payroll, et cetera.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:47.710
And along with cash
is king, there's

00:27:47.710 --> 00:27:52.510
another area that I think
is equally important.

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:54.980
And that is marketing is queen.

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:59.320
And without a king and queen,
you won't get there from here.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:03.400
You know, particularly
for technologists,

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:05.290
they think that they
have this hot idea,

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:07.300
and it probably is real
hot, and they maybe

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:10.160
have done their homework, et
cetera, in terms of technology.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:11.980
But I got to tell you,
these two elements

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:14.980
are absolutely necessary to get
there from here, particularly

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:15.940
the marketing.

00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:18.620
And of course, you've got to
have the cash for the payroll

00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:21.290
and so forth.

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:24.183
And finally, as I said earlier,
work for the achievement.

00:28:24.183 --> 00:28:25.350
Don't worry about the money.

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:27.280
If you do a good job,
the money will come.

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:31.947
And you can quote me.

00:28:31.947 --> 00:28:34.929
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:54.663 --> 00:28:55.830
PRESENTER: That was so cool.

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:03.260
Before we continue, we
need everybody to stand up.

00:29:03.260 --> 00:29:03.760
Up.

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:09.240
And look into the center
and see if there's

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:10.500
an empty seat next to you.

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:13.017
And if there is,
move to the center,

00:29:13.017 --> 00:29:14.850
because we have people
standing in the back,

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:18.440
and we'd like them to be able
to sit during the presentation.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:18.940
Here we go.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:20.640
Squish.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:22.050
All right, everybody in?

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:22.770
You can sit down.

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:32.584
That was our intermission.

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:43.760
We're up to the
part in your program

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:47.810
that says Organizing
Team Transition.

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:51.500
The competition is organized
by students for students

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:54.230
here at MIT, and we've
had a great team leading

