for one gift in a small store.

00:55:01.890 --> 00:55:05.370
They don't have to alert their
wedding guests which stores

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:07.680
are registered in, because
the wedding guests can

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:10.200
go to the internet and purchase
the gifts whenever they

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:12.750
want right over the internet.

00:55:12.750 --> 00:55:15.630
They can also purchase the gifts
from the store if they want.

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:18.593
While on the internet, they
can search by gift type

00:55:18.593 --> 00:55:20.010
if they want to
just get something

00:55:20.010 --> 00:55:21.868
in the linens category,
or they can also

00:55:21.868 --> 00:55:23.910
search by costs, if they're
only willing to spend

00:55:23.910 --> 00:55:26.160
a certain amount per gift.

00:55:26.160 --> 00:55:27.750
Lastly, the retail stores.

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:29.920
The retail stores,
especially the smaller ones

00:55:29.920 --> 00:55:32.330
and the ones that currently
don't have registry systems

00:55:32.330 --> 00:55:36.060
will have an enormously
expanded customer base.

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:37.560
Obviously, with the
internet, people

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:39.690
who aren't located
near the retail stores

00:55:39.690 --> 00:55:41.970
will be able to just,
again, go on the internet

00:55:41.970 --> 00:55:44.010
and purchase gifts from there.

00:55:44.010 --> 00:55:47.280
Therefore, WeddingBell.com is
the entire registry solution,

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:51.297
the online solution and
the in-store solution.

00:55:51.297 --> 00:55:53.880
Our business model, this is the
way we're going to make money.

00:55:53.880 --> 00:55:55.980
We've done some surveying, and
we've talked to some industry

00:55:55.980 --> 00:55:56.670
experts.

00:55:56.670 --> 00:55:59.280
And we've found that
the retail stores

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:01.590
are willing to pay a setup
fee and a maintenance fee.

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:04.170
They're also willing to
pay a percentage of sales.

00:56:04.170 --> 00:56:05.470
This helps us a lot.

00:56:05.470 --> 00:56:08.230
We get fees from them
in two different ways.

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:12.540
The wedding guests-- that's the
little yellow people there--

00:56:12.540 --> 00:56:17.070
we're going to charge them a
surcharge, only about $2.95.

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:19.260
You might find
yourself saying, well,

00:56:19.260 --> 00:56:20.675
would I be willing to pay that?

00:56:20.675 --> 00:56:22.800
But if you think about when
you buy a wedding gift,

00:56:22.800 --> 00:56:24.900
you really have a
gift giving threshold

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:27.210
that you don't want
to spend more than.

00:56:27.210 --> 00:56:29.790
And as long as the gift you
buy falls within that range,

00:56:29.790 --> 00:56:31.623
we found that the wedding
guests we surveyed

00:56:31.623 --> 00:56:33.310
would be willing to pay.

00:56:33.310 --> 00:56:35.520
Also note that we're not
charging the engaged couple.

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:37.350
If anyone out there is
getting married, don't worry,

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:39.300
you won't have to pay
a cent for our service.

00:56:39.300 --> 00:56:42.248
A lot of our competitors
charge you, however.

00:56:42.248 --> 00:56:44.040
We also found that the
engaged couples just

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:45.010
don't think it's fair.

00:56:45.010 --> 00:56:45.870
It's their day.

00:56:45.870 --> 00:56:50.340
They don't want to be charged
to put gifts on their registry.

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:53.080
With any startup,
there are risks.

00:56:53.080 --> 00:56:56.190
So WeddingBell.com
has its risks as well.

00:56:56.190 --> 00:56:59.770
Our first risk is having
reliable system operation.

00:56:59.770 --> 00:57:01.920
A lot of people have
tried to buy gifts

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:04.290
from certain department
stores in the area,

00:57:04.290 --> 00:57:06.900
and they find that their wedding
registry systems are down,

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:09.610
or they can't get the printouts,
or they can't buy the gifts.

00:57:09.610 --> 00:57:12.180
Well, we're going to defend
that with redundant connectivity

00:57:12.180 --> 00:57:13.303
to the internet.

00:57:13.303 --> 00:57:14.970
We're also going to
have local front end

00:57:14.970 --> 00:57:17.687
software that the retailers
will have in their store,

00:57:17.687 --> 00:57:19.770
so that even if the internet
connection goes down,

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:23.920
they'll still be able to enter
all of their registry orders.

00:57:23.920 --> 00:57:26.580
We realize also that
obtaining customer commitment

00:57:26.580 --> 00:57:30.610
is vital to the success of
WeddingBell.com as a business.

00:57:30.610 --> 00:57:32.400
But we've developed
a marketing plan

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:34.230
and through our
promotions and our PR,

00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:36.660
we plan to get the wedding
guests, the engaged couple,

00:57:36.660 --> 00:57:39.660
and the retail stores to buy
into this network all at once

00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:41.862
and have it grow
extremely rapidly.

00:57:41.862 --> 00:57:43.320
Finally, we know
that this business

00:57:43.320 --> 00:57:44.800
has some low barriers to entry.

00:57:44.800 --> 00:57:47.670
It's not that difficult
for people to get into it,

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:52.020
but we plan to combat this by
having a first mover advantage.

00:57:52.020 --> 00:57:55.290
We want to quickly
establish our network,

00:57:55.290 --> 00:57:57.570
and we plan to do that
through our business model

00:57:57.570 --> 00:58:01.837
and getting the engaged
couples and the retail stores

00:58:01.837 --> 00:58:04.170
as well as wedding guests all
into this at the same time

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:06.390
and through a very
specific entry strategy.

00:58:06.390 --> 00:58:09.118
We hope to raise
that entry barrier.

00:58:09.118 --> 00:58:11.160
Finally, Steve is going
to come back and give you

00:58:11.160 --> 00:58:13.085
some concluding remarks.

00:58:13.085 --> 00:58:14.460
STEVEN ROSENBLATT:
Thanks, Peter.

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:16.650
So why is WeddingBell.com
ready to go?

00:58:16.650 --> 00:58:20.318
First off www.WeddingBell.com
is registered with InterNIC.

00:58:20.318 --> 00:58:22.110
We could already
establish a brand identity

00:58:22.110 --> 00:58:23.550
through this URL.

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:25.440
Second, we already
have five retail stores

00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:27.030
have given preliminary
commitments

00:58:27.030 --> 00:58:29.382
to have the system
installed into their stores.

00:58:29.382 --> 00:58:30.840
Third, as I mentioned,
the timing's

00:58:30.840 --> 00:58:33.030
right with the internet
and e-commerce boom.

00:58:33.030 --> 00:58:35.070
And finally, we just
have a dedicated team

00:58:35.070 --> 00:58:37.028
of really good people
that are really committed

00:58:37.028 --> 00:58:37.830
to this project.

00:58:37.830 --> 00:58:40.230
WeddingBell.com, we're ready
to revolutionize the wedding

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:41.260
industry today.

00:58:41.260 --> 00:58:41.760
Thank you.

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:44.190
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:52.987 --> 00:58:54.820
SCOTT BLANKSTEEN: Thank
you very much, guys.

00:58:54.820 --> 00:58:56.680
OK, the next team is
something very special.

00:58:56.680 --> 00:58:58.660
It's a Volunteer
Community Connection.

00:58:58.660 --> 00:58:59.950
And here they are now.

00:59:22.880 --> 00:59:24.440
JOHN: Hi, I'm John,
and this is Mike.

00:59:24.440 --> 00:59:26.565
And we're here to talk to
you about revolutionizing

00:59:26.565 --> 00:59:28.010
volunteerism.

00:59:28.010 --> 00:59:30.050
MIKE: Non-profit
agencies and volunteers

00:59:30.050 --> 00:59:32.840
have thus far failed to
converge on the internet.

00:59:32.840 --> 00:59:35.030
We offer an online
solution to bridge

00:59:35.030 --> 00:59:38.480
the gap between these volunteers
and nonprofit agencies.

00:59:38.480 --> 00:59:41.360
Volunteers will now
be able to easily find

00:59:41.360 --> 00:59:43.370
the opportunities for
which they are looking,

00:59:43.370 --> 00:59:46.490
and nonprofit agencies
will be able to efficiently

00:59:46.490 --> 00:59:49.370
recruit and manage the
volunteers that they need.

00:59:49.370 --> 00:59:52.610
This potential market
is, simply put, huge.

00:59:52.610 --> 00:59:55.240
There are 50 million Americans
who volunteered last year.

00:59:55.240 --> 00:59:58.280
25% of these Americans
are already online.

00:59:58.280 --> 01:00:00.350
And as you've heard many
times today, this number

01:00:00.350 --> 01:00:02.330
is doubling in the
next two years.

01:00:02.330 --> 01:00:04.055
There are over 10,000
large corporations

01:00:04.055 --> 01:00:05.930
who are just now beginning
to develop company

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:07.760
wide volunteering efforts.

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:10.550
At $450 per month, this
represents a total market

01:00:10.550 --> 01:00:11.390
of over $50 million.

01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:14.510
And there over
550,000 nonprofits

01:00:14.510 --> 01:00:17.060
looking to recruit
and manage volunteers.

01:00:17.060 --> 01:00:19.070
At $25 per month,
this represents

01:00:19.070 --> 01:00:20.810
a market of over $160 million.

01:00:24.860 --> 01:00:27.470
This market has been
unaddressed today.

01:00:27.470 --> 01:00:29.690
The internet offers only
static and passive access

01:00:29.690 --> 01:00:31.400
to volunteers.

01:00:31.400 --> 01:00:34.650
Nonprofit agencies can only
list things on the internet.

01:00:34.650 --> 01:00:36.530
There is no active
recruitment of volunteers,

01:00:36.530 --> 01:00:38.030
and there is no management.

01:00:38.030 --> 01:00:41.545
As a result, volunteers of
today are alone and confused.

01:00:46.020 --> 01:00:48.360
We offer the solution.

01:00:48.360 --> 01:00:50.670
The VCC solves the
problems of volunteerism

01:00:50.670 --> 01:00:54.480
for volunteers, nonprofit
agencies, and corporations.

01:00:54.480 --> 01:00:56.820
Volunteers will be able to
find the right opportunities

01:00:56.820 --> 01:00:58.530
for them, simply
by saying things

01:00:58.530 --> 01:01:01.080
like, I want to
volunteer with education,

01:01:01.080 --> 01:01:03.650
with kids, clicking
Search, and it's done.

01:01:03.650 --> 01:01:07.650
Nonprofit agencies will be
able to recruit volunteers

01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:09.990
on the internet, offline,
online, together,

01:01:09.990 --> 01:01:11.328
and effectively manage them.

01:01:11.328 --> 01:01:12.870
And corporations
will all of a sudden

01:01:12.870 --> 01:01:15.450
have an easy way to begin
company wide volunteering

01:01:15.450 --> 01:01:17.250
efforts.

01:01:17.250 --> 01:01:20.630
How does this work for you
and me, the volunteers?

01:01:20.630 --> 01:01:22.770
We log into the VCC website.

01:01:22.770 --> 01:01:24.660
And so we select
what we want to do.

01:01:24.660 --> 01:01:25.900
What could be easier?

01:01:25.900 --> 01:01:27.042
We hit Search.

01:01:27.042 --> 01:01:28.500
And right away, we
see the agencies

01:01:28.500 --> 01:01:29.970
that offer the opportunities.

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:34.990
We are a nonprofit organization.

01:01:34.990 --> 01:01:36.880
And as a nonprofit,
our primary goal

01:01:36.880 --> 01:01:39.910
is to serve the agencies and
volunteers in our communities.

01:01:39.910 --> 01:01:41.410
For the agencies
in our communities,

01:01:41.410 --> 01:01:44.620
we recruit groups of volunteers,
whom we call the web tutors.

01:01:44.620 --> 01:01:47.090
Web tutors work at
nonprofit agencies,

01:01:47.090 --> 01:01:49.870
teaching them about
technology, about the internet,

01:01:49.870 --> 01:01:51.170
and about email.

01:01:51.170 --> 01:01:53.420
These are things that some
of us may think are normal,

01:01:53.420 --> 01:01:55.480
but nonprofit
agencies have thus far

01:01:55.480 --> 01:01:58.810
failed to achieve the resources
to learn these technologies.

01:01:58.810 --> 01:02:01.090
We recruit volunteers to
show them why it is useful

01:02:01.090 --> 01:02:02.540
and how to use it.

01:02:02.540 --> 01:02:05.110
We also run volunteer fairs,
conferences, seminars,

01:02:05.110 --> 01:02:07.030
online forums,
activities to link

01:02:07.030 --> 01:02:10.565
nonprofit agencies and us, the
volunteers, together in person.

01:02:10.565 --> 01:02:12.190
JOHN: But the reason
we're here tonight

01:02:12.190 --> 01:02:14.890
is because of the services
we're now planning

01:02:14.890 --> 01:02:17.680
on offering to our agencies.

01:02:17.680 --> 01:02:19.960
An example of our
value proposition.

01:02:19.960 --> 01:02:21.970
We look at Boston
Cares, a medium

01:02:21.970 --> 01:02:24.010
sized nonprofit in
the Boston area.

01:02:24.010 --> 01:02:26.770
They send out monthly mailings
to about 2,000 volunteers,

01:02:26.770 --> 01:02:29.518
spending overall $7,000 a year.

01:02:29.518 --> 01:02:31.060
It's really just a
shock and approach

01:02:31.060 --> 01:02:32.290
to their 2,000 volunteers.

01:02:32.290 --> 01:02:33.790
And it's only once
a month a mailing

01:02:33.790 --> 01:02:37.300
of about 50 opportunities,
not targeted at all.

01:02:37.300 --> 01:02:38.980
We offer something
completely different.

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:43.180
For only $25 a month, we
offer targeted e-mailings.

01:02:43.180 --> 01:02:47.450
We offer unlimited, basically,
access to our volunteer base.

01:02:47.450 --> 01:02:49.000
So if we have
100,000 volunteers,

01:02:49.000 --> 01:02:50.470
they really only target 10%.

01:02:50.470 --> 01:02:54.270
But those 10% are committed
volunteers to their cause.

01:02:54.270 --> 01:02:57.640
And we do this constantly
as they add new projects,

01:02:57.640 --> 01:03:02.020
saving them $6,700
or 95% of which I

01:03:02.020 --> 01:03:05.680
guess you could call their
volunteer acquisition cost.

01:03:05.680 --> 01:03:08.800
We're planning on taking this
value proposition nationally.

01:03:08.800 --> 01:03:13.090
Starting out slowly this year,
recruiting 1,000 agencies

01:03:13.090 --> 01:03:15.130
and about 39
corporations, moving

01:03:15.130 --> 01:03:19.690
to 37,000 agencies and 1,000
corporations in our fifth year.

01:03:19.690 --> 01:03:21.850
And that's only
4% of the market,

01:03:21.850 --> 01:03:23.860
because this market is huge.

01:03:23.860 --> 01:03:25.540
In order to expand
to these numbers,

01:03:25.540 --> 01:03:28.210
we need to concentrate
on two things--

01:03:28.210 --> 01:03:29.760
our sales and marketing efforts.

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:31.510
In order to do this,
we're going to target

01:03:31.510 --> 01:03:33.670
three specific markets--

01:03:33.670 --> 01:03:35.740
volunteers, agencies,
and corporations.

01:03:35.740 --> 01:03:38.680
We're going to target
these through three

01:03:38.680 --> 01:03:40.210
very different ways.

01:03:40.210 --> 01:03:42.100
For volunteers,
we're going to use

01:03:42.100 --> 01:03:45.640
online ads, banner ads on Yahoo,
Excite, and other portals.

01:03:45.640 --> 01:03:48.250
For agencies, we're going
to use direct mail and phone

01:03:48.250 --> 01:03:51.880
contact, utilizing the IRS
database of over 700,000

01:03:51.880 --> 01:03:53.650
nonprofits in the United States.

01:03:53.650 --> 01:03:55.308
And lastly, corporations.

01:03:55.308 --> 01:03:57.100
We're going to dispatch
people from the VCC

01:03:57.100 --> 01:03:59.770
to contact the CEOs
and HR people directly

01:03:59.770 --> 01:04:03.430
to get involved in their
corporate volunteer Programs

01:04:03.430 --> 01:04:06.267
As we add more agencies
and more volunteers,

01:04:06.267 --> 01:04:08.600
we need to have a management
staff in place that's going

01:04:08.600 --> 01:04:09.910
to be able to handle this.

01:04:09.910 --> 01:04:12.220
And currently, we have a
dedicated management team.

01:04:12.220 --> 01:04:14.260
We have Emily Sandberg
on our board helping

01:04:14.260 --> 01:04:16.002
to drive and direct the VCC.

01:04:16.002 --> 01:04:18.460
We have Rich [? Sanford, ?]
one of the founders of the VCC,

01:04:18.460 --> 01:04:21.340
who's in direct contact with
the agencies on a daily basis,

01:04:21.340 --> 01:04:22.487
identifying their needs.

01:04:22.487 --> 01:04:24.820
Mike [? Bryzyk ?] and [? Umi ?]
[? Mehrotra ?] developed

01:04:24.820 --> 01:04:27.460
our software and have now split
up with Mike working on sales

01:04:27.460 --> 01:04:29.793
and marketing and [? Umi ?]
developing the final release

01:04:29.793 --> 01:04:30.477
of the software.

01:04:30.477 --> 01:04:32.560
And I'm John, and I'm
responsible for the business

01:04:32.560 --> 01:04:36.250
plan and focusing on
sustainability for the VCC.

01:04:36.250 --> 01:04:38.710
And the VCC has been running
for about a year now.

01:04:38.710 --> 01:04:41.590
And in order to reach a
level of sustainability,

01:04:41.590 --> 01:04:44.320
we think we need to focus on
the following three steps--

01:04:44.320 --> 01:04:47.170
funding, management,
and partnerships.

01:04:47.170 --> 01:04:48.400
Let's start with funding.

01:04:48.400 --> 01:04:51.280
The 50K is really just a
stepping stone for the VCC.

01:04:51.280 --> 01:04:52.990
After the 50K, we're
go and approach

01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:55.390
larger foundations
such as the Boston

01:04:55.390 --> 01:04:57.588
Foundation for larger grants.

01:04:57.588 --> 01:05:00.130
At the same time, we're going
to be working on our management

01:05:00.130 --> 01:05:03.040
team, recruiting a CEO,
someone with national volunteer

01:05:03.040 --> 01:05:04.810
or nonprofit experience
or maybe someone

01:05:04.810 --> 01:05:08.357
from the corporate world to
help us take this nationally.

01:05:08.357 --> 01:05:10.690
We're going to work on our
sales and marketing materials

01:05:10.690 --> 01:05:13.060
and work on the final
release of the VCS software

01:05:13.060 --> 01:05:15.017
for release in September.

01:05:15.017 --> 01:05:16.600
And lastly, while
we're doing all this

01:05:16.600 --> 01:05:18.965
we're constantly
working on partnerships,

01:05:18.965 --> 01:05:20.590
online partnerships
such as boston.com,

01:05:20.590 --> 01:05:24.070
university partnerships, such
as Boston College, Boston

01:05:24.070 --> 01:05:26.080
University, and
community partnerships,

01:05:26.080 --> 01:05:27.070
like Boston Cares.

01:05:29.710 --> 01:05:31.780
Achieving these three
steps will allow

01:05:31.780 --> 01:05:35.600
us to reach our goal of
revolutionizing volunteerism.

01:05:35.600 --> 01:05:38.960
The VCC services have
never been offered before.

01:05:38.960 --> 01:05:41.330
We increase the ease
of volunteering,

01:05:41.330 --> 01:05:43.970
and we increase the
satisfaction of volunteering

01:05:43.970 --> 01:05:47.570
because we offer volunteers
targeted positions

01:05:47.570 --> 01:05:48.840
at these agencies.

01:05:48.840 --> 01:05:51.400
And we've been growing
steadily since last year.

01:05:51.400 --> 01:05:54.860
And additional funding at
this key moment for the VCC

01:05:54.860 --> 01:05:57.800
will allow us to launch in
September and reach our goal.

01:05:57.800 --> 01:05:58.638
Thank you.

01:05:58.638 --> 01:06:01.566
[APPLAUSE]

01:06:10.560 --> 01:06:11.560
SCOTT BLANKSTEEN: Great.

01:06:11.560 --> 01:06:12.580
And let's see.

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:14.440
We're two thirds of
the way there now.

01:06:14.440 --> 01:06:15.910
We've got two more teams to go.

01:06:18.880 --> 01:06:22.791
Next team, Akamai Technologies.

01:06:32.863 --> 01:06:34.280
JONATHAN SEELIG:
Hi, good evening.

01:06:34.280 --> 01:06:36.238
I'm very honored to be
here representing Akamai

01:06:36.238 --> 01:06:37.921
technologies [INAUDIBLE].

01:06:57.590 --> 01:06:58.100
Perfect.

01:06:58.100 --> 01:06:58.600
Look good?

01:07:01.422 --> 01:07:03.380
I'm very honored to be
here representing Akamai

01:07:03.380 --> 01:07:04.463
Technologies this evening.

01:07:16.320 --> 01:07:18.280
Couple of
technological glitches.

01:07:21.970 --> 01:07:25.390
My name is Jonathan Seelig
I'm a first year MBA student

01:07:25.390 --> 01:07:27.370
at the Sloan School at MIT.

01:07:27.370 --> 01:07:30.670
And I wish to present to
you global hosting servers.

01:07:30.670 --> 01:07:33.370
Akamai Technology
intend to change the way

01:07:33.370 --> 01:07:36.220
that content is distributed
throughout the internet.

01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:38.747
I have three things that I'd
like to present to you tonight.

