01:17:03.865 --> 01:17:05.740
GARY CULLISS: We are
Direct Hit Technologies,

01:17:05.740 --> 01:17:08.470
and we are a company that
takes existing search engines

01:17:08.470 --> 01:17:09.707
that you use on the internet.

01:17:09.707 --> 01:17:11.290
We make them a lot
better, so that you

01:17:11.290 --> 01:17:15.418
can stop looking for information
and start finding it.

01:17:15.418 --> 01:17:17.210
The problem is that
with the internet being

01:17:17.210 --> 01:17:19.550
such a huge mass of data,
it's tough to locate

01:17:19.550 --> 01:17:21.200
relevant information.

01:17:21.200 --> 01:17:22.880
As you all know,
search engines often

01:17:22.880 --> 01:17:24.870
return a lot of
irrelevant results.

01:17:24.870 --> 01:17:27.710
And in fact, 90% of all
users in a recent survey

01:17:27.710 --> 01:17:30.350
have said that they want
more targeted search results.

01:17:30.350 --> 01:17:31.850
So this problem is
only to get worse

01:17:31.850 --> 01:17:33.770
as the internet
continues to grow.

01:17:33.770 --> 01:17:36.882
It's currently estimated to be
about 300 million documents.

01:17:36.882 --> 01:17:38.840
And that number is expected
to exceed 1 billion

01:17:38.840 --> 01:17:42.180
by the year 2002.

01:17:42.180 --> 01:17:43.950
The way current
search engines work,

01:17:43.950 --> 01:17:46.530
take Infoseek, Alta Vista,
Excite, the other search

01:17:46.530 --> 01:17:48.600
engines, they look
to the keywords

01:17:48.600 --> 01:17:50.760
that authors put in the
document in the meta tags

01:17:50.760 --> 01:17:53.050
to provide the
relevancy rankings.

01:17:53.050 --> 01:17:55.560
This is the position of the
search result in the list

01:17:55.560 --> 01:17:56.765
that you get back.

01:17:56.765 --> 01:17:58.140
The problem with
this is that you

01:17:58.140 --> 01:17:59.970
can get a lot of
irrelevant documents,

01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:02.370
and quite frankly, you
get too many results.

01:18:02.370 --> 01:18:04.840
So the editors at Yahoo
have a different approach.

01:18:04.840 --> 01:18:07.730
They actually have a staff of
people surfing the internet

01:18:07.730 --> 01:18:09.090
and looking for websites.

01:18:09.090 --> 01:18:12.600
They classify them according
to categories and keywords.

01:18:12.600 --> 01:18:16.090
Problem with this is that it can
be labor intensive, expensive,

01:18:16.090 --> 01:18:18.090
and quite frankly, they're
struggling to keep up

01:18:18.090 --> 01:18:19.338
with the growth in the web.

01:18:19.338 --> 01:18:20.880
Right now, they have
approximately 1%

01:18:20.880 --> 01:18:22.507
of the internet indexed.

01:18:22.507 --> 01:18:24.840
Direct Hit is going to take
a totally different approach

01:18:24.840 --> 01:18:25.890
to this problem.

01:18:25.890 --> 01:18:28.170
We're going to actually
empower the users to organize

01:18:28.170 --> 01:18:29.463
the search results.

01:18:29.463 --> 01:18:31.380
Our rankings are based
on what people actually

01:18:31.380 --> 01:18:32.820
select from the hit list.

01:18:32.820 --> 01:18:35.010
So what you get is a
market for information,

01:18:35.010 --> 01:18:38.280
where the sites that people
select move up in the rankings.

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:41.990
The ones that they ignore
move down in the rankings.

01:18:41.990 --> 01:18:44.510
So our premise is that with
each search engine being visited

01:18:44.510 --> 01:18:47.007
by approximately 20
million people a day,

01:18:47.007 --> 01:18:49.340
there's a high likelihood
that somebody else has already

01:18:49.340 --> 01:18:51.560
found what you're looking for.

01:18:51.560 --> 01:18:53.930
Direct Hit allows you the 20
million searchers out there

01:18:53.930 --> 01:18:56.600
to automatically rank
those documents for us.

01:18:56.600 --> 01:19:00.190
You don't have to do anything
that you're not already doing.

01:19:00.190 --> 01:19:02.140
Now, how does all this work?

01:19:02.140 --> 01:19:05.230
The searches are re-ranked
by what searchers like.

01:19:05.230 --> 01:19:07.630
We have proprietary
software algorithms

01:19:07.630 --> 01:19:10.120
that know exactly what sites
previous searchers liked

01:19:10.120 --> 01:19:11.095
and did not like.

01:19:11.095 --> 01:19:12.220
And it's totally automatic.

01:19:12.220 --> 01:19:13.845
The searchers don't
have to do anything

01:19:13.845 --> 01:19:15.313
that they're not already doing.

01:19:15.313 --> 01:19:16.730
The result, as I
mentioned before,

01:19:16.730 --> 01:19:18.105
is a market for
information where

01:19:18.105 --> 01:19:20.080
the good stuff rises to
the top, the bad stuff

01:19:20.080 --> 01:19:22.475
sinks to the bottom.

01:19:22.475 --> 01:19:24.350
Let's take a look at a
current search engine.

01:19:24.350 --> 01:19:26.720
This is a search query
for Boston MBTA, which

01:19:26.720 --> 01:19:28.237
is a local transit authority.

01:19:28.237 --> 01:19:30.320
People go to the site to
look for subway schedules

01:19:30.320 --> 01:19:31.850
and commuter rail schedules.

01:19:31.850 --> 01:19:34.010
And you can see that
this is actual results

01:19:34.010 --> 01:19:35.960
from a current search
engine on the internet,

01:19:35.960 --> 01:19:38.060
and it's giving pretty
irrelevant results.

01:19:38.060 --> 01:19:40.900
You've got Boston apartments,
internet search tools,

01:19:40.900 --> 01:19:43.310
the Hong Kong students
will know about and Boston

01:19:43.310 --> 01:19:46.450
links for information.

01:19:46.450 --> 01:19:48.713
With the Direct Hit
technology, by monitoring

01:19:48.713 --> 01:19:50.380
what people actually
pick from the list,

01:19:50.380 --> 01:19:52.810
we can provide much
better relevancy ranking.

01:19:52.810 --> 01:19:54.650
You can see here that
the official website

01:19:54.650 --> 01:19:57.370
of the MBTA, which
is the best hit

01:19:57.370 --> 01:20:01.000
is actually listed number one,
along with a few other highly

01:20:01.000 --> 01:20:02.088
relevant documents.

01:20:02.088 --> 01:20:03.880
These are actual results
that are generated

01:20:03.880 --> 01:20:06.840
with our current prototype.

01:20:06.840 --> 01:20:08.530
So how do we make
money from all this?

01:20:08.530 --> 01:20:10.110
Well, our business
model is not to compete

01:20:10.110 --> 01:20:12.750
with the search engines, but in
fact, to partner up with them.

01:20:12.750 --> 01:20:14.940
We've already got two
of the four major search

01:20:14.940 --> 01:20:18.055
engines who have agreed
to add a link to our site.

01:20:18.055 --> 01:20:20.430
And what's going to happen is
if a user types in a search

01:20:20.430 --> 01:20:22.800
query and gets results
that they're unhappy with,

01:20:22.800 --> 01:20:24.570
they can click on our
button, a direct hit

01:20:24.570 --> 01:20:27.090
will deliver a page
that's re-ranked by what

01:20:27.090 --> 01:20:29.460
other people have actually
selected for a similar search

01:20:29.460 --> 01:20:30.132
query.

01:20:30.132 --> 01:20:32.340
We're going to be co-branded
with the existing search

01:20:32.340 --> 01:20:33.053
portal.

01:20:33.053 --> 01:20:34.470
And the way we
actually make money

01:20:34.470 --> 01:20:35.790
is we present a new ad banner.

01:20:35.790 --> 01:20:37.498
We've created some
additional real estate

01:20:37.498 --> 01:20:39.840
that we can sell ad
banners on, and that we

01:20:39.840 --> 01:20:41.550
have an ad revenue
sharing agreement

01:20:41.550 --> 01:20:43.728
with our search partners.

01:20:43.728 --> 01:20:45.270
Additional markets
for the technology

01:20:45.270 --> 01:20:46.830
include search driven
commerce sites,

01:20:46.830 --> 01:20:50.820
like netmarket.com and Chemdex
which sell specialty chemicals.

01:20:50.820 --> 01:20:53.850
Corporate internet document
servers can also benefit,

01:20:53.850 --> 01:20:57.000
and smaller website owners who
we can sell separate software

01:20:57.000 --> 01:20:59.810
for their search driven sites.

01:20:59.810 --> 01:21:02.000
Our competitive
advantages include

01:21:02.000 --> 01:21:05.060
our current strategic
alliances with two engines.

01:21:05.060 --> 01:21:06.802
We are complementary,
our technology.

01:21:06.802 --> 01:21:08.510
It's not competitive
with search engines,

01:21:08.510 --> 01:21:10.700
because users can choose
between the pure search

01:21:10.700 --> 01:21:13.850
results and the results that
other people have found.

01:21:13.850 --> 01:21:16.520
We're going to be the first to
the market with the technology.

01:21:16.520 --> 01:21:18.110
Our business model
is technically

01:21:18.110 --> 01:21:20.540
and financially risk free
to the search engines,

01:21:20.540 --> 01:21:22.940
doesn't interfere with their
existing legacy systems.

01:21:22.940 --> 01:21:25.190
We have a competitive pricing
because all of our deals

01:21:25.190 --> 01:21:26.210
are non-exclusive.

01:21:26.210 --> 01:21:27.680
And if none of that
works, we still

01:21:27.680 --> 01:21:30.207
have four patents pending.

01:21:30.207 --> 01:21:32.540
And with that, I'm going to
turn it over to Mike Cassidy

01:21:32.540 --> 01:21:35.620
to explain the numbers
bind the business.

01:21:35.620 --> 01:21:36.985
MIKE CASSIDY: Thanks, Gary.

01:21:36.985 --> 01:21:38.860
The important thing to
notice from this chart

01:21:38.860 --> 01:21:42.642
is actually the number of daily
page views being generated,

01:21:42.642 --> 01:21:44.350
including Alta Vista,
which is not shown,

01:21:44.350 --> 01:21:46.227
today there being
120 million page

01:21:46.227 --> 01:21:48.810
views a day, 120 million times
people are searching and seeing

01:21:48.810 --> 01:21:51.690
result. And the growth
rate is incredible,

01:21:51.690 --> 01:21:53.470
between 100 and 200% per year.

01:21:57.750 --> 01:21:59.490
The key for our
revenue generation

01:21:59.490 --> 01:22:02.790
is what fraction of those
pages we can capture.

01:22:02.790 --> 01:22:05.550
This model shows that
by the end of the year,

01:22:05.550 --> 01:22:06.800
there'll be 200 million pages.

01:22:06.800 --> 01:22:09.470
If we can capture 30 million
of those with our partner,

01:22:09.470 --> 01:22:12.060
and if we can get a 25%
ad share revenue, which

01:22:12.060 --> 01:22:13.470
is quite typical
in the industry,

01:22:13.470 --> 01:22:16.050
typically we partner up
with Alta Vista or someone

01:22:16.050 --> 01:22:19.410
like that, they will take 20%
off the top from the ad revenue

01:22:19.410 --> 01:22:21.300
and then split the
remainder half and half

01:22:21.300 --> 01:22:22.900
between us and them.

01:22:22.900 --> 01:22:24.840
So if we can do
even less than that

01:22:24.840 --> 01:22:28.560
and assuming a much lower
ad revenue also, typically

01:22:28.560 --> 01:22:31.890
in the industry today, you'll
be able to charge $50 to $60

01:22:31.890 --> 01:22:33.900
per 1,000 for keyword search.

01:22:33.900 --> 01:22:36.000
We're only assuming
$20 per 1,000.

01:22:36.000 --> 01:22:38.520
We can quickly generate, as
you can see, $30 to $40 million

01:22:38.520 --> 01:22:40.458
worth of revenue.

01:22:40.458 --> 01:22:42.250
I just want to talk
